{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +1.5 €/t early horizon; ≈ +2 to +4 €/t mid/late horizon).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the recent history turns upward (post-dip rebound), the model reacts too slowly and misses the subsequent rally. Add a momentum term: if last 5–10 days slope > 0, increase forecasts progressively with horizon (larger uplift after day ~7).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error increases with lead time (near-term errors small; 2–4 week horizon errors large). Use a ramping correction: uplift grows roughly linearly with horizon (e.g., +0.05 to +0.15 €/t per forecast day).",
    "Underpricing of high-price regime: once spot moves into ~70–73 range, the model mean-reverts too low (anchors around high-60s). Impose regime adjustment: if last close > 70, shift the whole path upward and reduce mean-reversion strength.",
    "Insufficient spike capture: during sharp upward moves, model peaks are too low and too early (damped amplitude). Inflate volatility/peak amplitude conditional on recent large positive returns (e.g., if any of last 3 daily returns > +1.5, add extra +1 to +2 €/t around days 5–15).",
    "Late-horizon overshoot risk after extreme late spikes (seen in one case): if model projects a steep late surge (> +3 €/t over 5 days) without matching momentum in the last 10 days of history, cap the late-horizon slope (soft ceiling) to avoid unrealistic blow-off tops."
  ]
}
